# LEARN[02]: why this repo lives on the WSL2 native filesystem, not /mnt/c  (deep-dive — D17)
# Why this way: the project root is ~/dev2/Sentinel on the ext4 volume *inside* the WSL2 VM.
#   `/mnt/c/...` is a Windows drive mounted by the 9P file protocol, so every open/read/write
#   syscall crosses the WSL2<->Windows VM boundary. Build/test tooling (uv sync, pytest
#   collection, ruff) issues thousands of small file ops, and the 9P overhead is per-syscall —
#   multiplicative, not a one-time setup cost. The Windows<->WSL2 boundary also silently
#   rewrites line endings and drops the executable bit.
# Good sides:
#   - uv sync / pytest / ruff run at native ext4 speed (no 9P round-trip per file)
#   - no CRLF translation: files stay LF, so shell scripts and git hooks keep working
#   - POSIX mode bits survive, so scripts/*.sh and the pre-commit protocol hook keep +x
# Drawbacks:
#   - the repo is invisible to Windows-native tools that don't understand WSL paths
#   - it lives entirely inside the WSL2 disk image (VHDX), so it is not in the Windows file tree
#   - editing from Windows requires the editor's WSL remote mode, not a plain Windows path
# Concept: /mnt/c is served by plan9 (9P), Microsoft's network-filesystem protocol used to
#   expose Windows drives into WSL2. It is not a local block device: each syscall becomes a
#   message round-trip to the host. ext4, by contrast, is the VM's own journaling filesystem —
#   real metadata + data I/O with no IPC. Two Windows semantics bite us here. (1) CRLF: a
#   Windows-global git config with core.autocrlf=true rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout and back
#   on commit, which corrupts shell scripts and pre-commit hooks; we set core.autocrlf=false
#   locally. (2) NTFS mode bits: NTFS has no POSIX execute bit, so files that should be
#   executable silently lose +x when created on Windows; core.filemode=true makes git track
#   mode-bit changes. Both are set explicitly locally to override any shared %USERPROFILE%
#   .gitconfig leaking in from Windows. The pre-commit hook that runs the protocol checker is a
#   shell script — if it lost LF or +x it would simply not run, making GW-1..GW-4 silently
#   unenforced. That is why the location decision is load-bearing, not a taste preference.
# See also: LEARN[01] (packaging/sync), and the GW-1..GW-4 clauses of the Working Protocol
# --- ignore rules below ---
# Python / uv
.venv/
.uv-cache/
.uv-python/
.pre-commit-cache/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/

# Env & secrets
.env

# Tool caches
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.cache/

# OS / editor
*.swp
.DS_Store
